1 REFN jb_coekelberghs
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME Joannes Baptista /Coekelberghs/
2 GIVN Joannes Baptista
2 SURN Coekelberghs
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 29 JUL 1809
2 PLAC Bertem
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 NOV 1880
2 PLAC Bertem
1 OCCU landbouwer (farmer)
1 FAMS @F36@
1 FAMS @F191@
1 FAMC @F149@
1 NOTE Father of the bride in his daughter [[coekelberghs]]'s marriage act — Sint-Stevens-Woluwe
2 CONT nr. 6, 24 April 1873. A *landbouwer* of Bertem, present at the wedding and consenting to
2 CONT it, and recorded as unable to write or sign, *ongeletterd zijnde*.
2 CONT
2 CONT Named a second time, thirty years later, in the same daughter's remarriage act — Kraainem
2 CONT nr. 16, 6 November 1903 — which is where his death comes from: Bertem, 19 November 1880.
2 CONT Two acts three decades apart naming the same man as her father is what carries this link;
2 CONT the 1873 act alone would be a surname and a commune.
2 CONT
2 CONT `sup` rather than `doc`: both are AGATHA act analyses — the Rijksarchief's own Demogen
2 CONT transcriptions — and neither act has a scan on AGATHA or Open Archives.
2 CONT
2 CONT GRAFTED (2026-07-26) — his own marriage act, **Bertem nr. 8, 30 October 1838, read as the
2 CONT register image** (`fs-bertem-1838-marriage-register-nr8`, ark `3:1:9392-HG83-X`). It gives
2 CONT his birth, **Bertem 29 July 1809**, his trade at twenty-nine, *slagter*, and his parents
2 CONT [[henricus_coekelberghs]] and [[petronella_elseviers]] — both there in person, both
2 CONT *present en Consenteerende*, both unable to sign, as were the bridegroom, the bride and her
2 CONT parents. Banns 14 and 21 October; four witnesses, none stated as kin to either party. The
2 CONT image also corrects AGATHA's spelling: the ink reads **Coekelberghs**, with no *c* before
2 CONT the *k*.
2 CONT
2 CONT **The trade change was the objection, and it is now answered rather than waved away.**
2 CONT The previous note here withheld the graft because he is a *slagter* in 1838, 1853 and 1867
2 CONT but a *landbouwer* in 1858, 1861 and 1873. Two Bertem birth acts settle it: at his
2 CONT daughter's birth on 27 June 1853 he declares himself in person, aged 44, born at Bertem,
2 CONT *slagter*; at his son's on 15 January 1858 he declares himself again, aged 48, born at
2 CONT Bertem, *landbouwer*. The same man, self-declaring, on both sides of the change, five years
2 CONT apart, in independently drawn acts — and both ages agree with a birth in July 1809. It is
2 CONT one life, not two men, and an occupation was never a field that vetoes a match.
2 CONT
2 CONT The rival reading was checked and refuted too. The only other Coekelberghs marriage
2 CONT indexed at Bertem in these years is *Joanna Catharina Coeckelberghs*, 1 February 1837 —
2 CONT a woman born in 1797, daughter of an unrelated *Petrus* Coekelberghs who died in 1798. A
2 CONT coincidence of surname, not a sibling and not a rival groom.
2 CONT
2 CONT **Still `sup`, and deliberately so.** The image proves what the 1838 act says. It does not
2 CONT prove that its bridegroom is the man the 1873 and 1903 acts name as
2 CONT [[coekelberghs]]'s father — that bridge runs entirely through two AGATHA transcriptions
2 CONT with no scan behind them. A chain is as strong as its weakest link, and after
2 CONT docs/research-log.md §52 this project says so rather than rounding up.
2 CONT
2 CONT FRONTIER — his own birth act would prove the 1809 date instead of reporting it, and his
2 CONT death act (Bertem, 19 November 1880) would give his age and confirm his parents. Both are
2 CONT on FamilySearch film **DGS 004471767** (*Geboorten 1796–1870, Huwelijken 1796–1832,
2 CONT Overlijdens 1796–1842*), which is unindexed and bound in blocks by record type, so it is a
2 CONT browse. Better still: **Maria Theresia's own Bertem birth act of 22 November 1848** — that
2 CONT one act would tie this 1838 couple directly to the 1848 daughter and move the whole cluster
2 CONT off the transcriptions.
1 SOUR @S17@
2 QUAY 2
