1 REFN rene_j
2 TYPE project-id
1 NAME René /Janssens/
2 GIVN René
2 SURN Janssens
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 2000
2 PLAC Oostende
1 FAMS @F48@
1 NOTE A witloof (Belgian-endive / “wit goud”) farmer — the family grew the famous chicory around Zaventem (Vlaams-Brabant). René and Anna later moved to the coast (Middelkerke), and René died in Oostende (after 2000). His Janssens ancestry is not yet cracked — but NOT because of any hard privacy barrier. Belgium's civil-record publicity rule is graduated (births open at 100 yrs, marriages 75, deaths 50) AND, crucially, a direct descendant may obtain a relative's birth/marriage/death act at any age, so the documents that name René's parents ARE obtainable by the family. The reason online searching hasn't found him is mundane: (a) 'Janssens' is one of Belgium's commonest surnames, so with no anchor (his birth village or year, or a parent's name) his records can't be told apart from thousands of others; and (b) no descendant has published him in an online tree, and his post-2000 coast death notice isn't in the digitised obituary aggregators (inmemoriam, ingedachten, DELA all checked, July 2026). CONTRAST with his wife's side: Anna Van Craenenbroeck's Zaventem family WAS located (see anna_vc) because that surname is rare and village-specific. UNLOCKS for René: his birth village + approx year (enough to request the birth act and pull his parents), a bidprentje, or a relative's memory of his parents' names.
